WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.480
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:09.437 --> 00:00:11.270
JASON COHEN: So I've
been building companies

00:00:11.270 --> 00:00:14.420
for 20 years in Austin,
Texas, and currently,

00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:16.850
started a company
called WP Engine

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:18.680
about 9 and 1/2 years ago.

00:00:18.680 --> 00:00:21.710
And we're big users of the
Cloud, as you're about to see.

00:00:21.710 --> 00:00:26.480
And in particular, we've just
transformed our corporate data

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:30.290
and our processes around
there using technology

00:00:30.290 --> 00:00:32.030
from Google and some
code of our own,

00:00:32.030 --> 00:00:33.560
which I'm going to
show you both of.

00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:35.820
So hopefully this
is useful to you.

00:00:35.820 --> 00:00:38.570
So here's where you go, oh,
wow, your stats are so big.

00:00:38.570 --> 00:00:40.940
You guys are amazing
and successful.

00:00:40.940 --> 00:00:44.300
I think my favorite one here
is, 5% of every human who

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:47.120
is online today, which is
about 3.5 billion people,

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:49.680
come to one of our
properties every day.

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:53.120
And so what we do is, we are
the most used and most popular

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:57.350
platform for running WordPress
digital experiences--

00:00:57.350 --> 00:00:59.120
so WordPress being the
open source project

00:00:59.120 --> 00:01:02.810
that powers one third of
every domain on earth today.

00:01:02.810 --> 00:01:06.978
And we power a lot of the larger
sites that use WordPress there.

00:01:06.978 --> 00:01:07.770
So that's all cool.

00:01:07.770 --> 00:01:11.720
And we have more than a
third of a million sites

00:01:11.720 --> 00:01:13.698
running on Google Cloud.

00:01:13.698 --> 00:01:15.740
And also, I'm going to
show you this NASCAR slide

00:01:15.740 --> 00:01:17.640
of a lot of customers.

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:19.520
And the reason is
actually to impress you

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:22.370
because everyone's got logos.

00:01:22.370 --> 00:01:25.940
But rather, in the
context of corporate data,

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:27.620
it's very easy, as
engineers, to think,

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:29.720
I know, I'll just grab all
the data from all the places

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:31.887
and stick it somewhere, and
then we'll make reports.

00:01:31.887 --> 00:01:33.085
Duh, it's the obvious thing.

00:01:33.085 --> 00:01:34.460
And there are
various reasons why

00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:37.800
that's not as simple as
what the real problem is.

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:39.020
And this is one reason.

00:01:39.020 --> 00:01:41.930
Because a lot of these folks
have requirements on us

00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:44.580
about what we do
with their data.

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:46.920
And as you probably
know, starting in Europe,

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:49.460
but now here as
well, companies are

00:01:49.460 --> 00:01:52.700
getting more and more strict
about what their vendors are

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:55.122
able to do, what kinds
of security practices

00:01:55.122 --> 00:01:56.830
you have to have around
that, whether you

00:01:56.830 --> 00:01:59.240
need to do SOC 2 and that
sort of stuff, or what.

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:01.850
And so when we think about
collecting our corporate data,

00:02:01.850 --> 00:02:04.320
which, of course, includes
our customers' data

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:07.310
and data about
customers, we have

00:02:07.310 --> 00:02:10.490
to think about what restrictions
are they putting on us?

00:02:10.490 --> 00:02:12.140
What audits do they give us?

00:02:12.140 --> 00:02:14.540
Because we have to pass
those while still doing what

00:02:14.540 --> 00:02:15.740
we want to do with data.

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:17.240
That actually makes
it kind of hard.

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:20.820
Again, I'm going to show you
how we work through that.

00:02:20.820 --> 00:02:24.050
So we had a challenge for
ourselves recently, which is--

00:02:24.050 --> 00:02:27.110
we have about 100,000
customers, which is nice--

00:02:27.110 --> 00:02:30.710
and as we scale and grow
the number of customers,

00:02:30.710 --> 00:02:32.120
everything else scales--

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:36.530
servers and difficulties,
processes and corporate data.

00:02:36.530 --> 00:02:38.215
So we have this
company-wide mantra

00:02:38.215 --> 00:02:40.340
of, hey, how are we going
to support half a million

00:02:40.340 --> 00:02:43.170
customers, so about
5x where we are today?

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:46.880
And I think this is actually
an interesting pattern

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:48.770
to use, in general,
when thinking

00:02:48.770 --> 00:02:51.210
about how to plan ahead.

00:02:51.210 --> 00:02:52.730
So a lot of folks
just say, well,

00:02:52.730 --> 00:02:54.170
when something
breaks, we fix it.

00:02:54.170 --> 00:02:55.753
That's, of course,
not planning ahead.

00:02:55.753 --> 00:02:58.430
That's being always behind,
by definition, right?

00:02:58.430 --> 00:02:59.828
And the other way is to say--

00:02:59.828 --> 00:03:01.120
and as engineers, we like this.

00:03:01.120 --> 00:03:03.350
Well, what if it's 10
or 100 times bigger?

00:03:03.350 --> 00:03:04.655
What would we need to build?

00:03:04.655 --> 00:03:07.280
And that's fun because it's fun
to think about how to architect

00:03:07.280 --> 00:03:09.170
something that large.

00:03:09.170 --> 00:03:10.682
It's also not necessary.

00:03:10.682 --> 00:03:12.890
You're not going to be 100
times bigger anytime soon.

00:03:12.890 --> 00:03:14.750
Most things aren't
going to be, anyway.

00:03:14.750 --> 00:03:16.580
And so you end up
over-architecting.

00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:19.010
And so this kind of rule
of thumb of, maybe 5x,

00:03:19.010 --> 00:03:21.950
is, to me, a nice rule of
planning in general for stuff,

00:03:21.950 --> 00:03:23.303
whether data or otherwise.

00:03:23.303 --> 00:03:25.470
It's enough in the future
that you're looking ahead,

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:27.660
but not going crazy and
over-architecting stuff.

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:31.080
So that was sort of our
guiding principle there.

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:33.488
And so what we wanted
to do is say, all right,

00:03:33.488 --> 00:03:34.530
we've got corporate data.

00:03:34.530 --> 00:03:35.363
What does that mean?

00:03:35.363 --> 00:03:39.080
It means financial data right
off of our general ledger

00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:41.390
or the invoices that we
charge customers every month.

00:03:41.390 --> 00:03:45.800
Again, we process about
70,000 invoices a month

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:48.830
because most customers
are month to month.

00:03:48.830 --> 00:03:51.260
And then there's data
about our user portal.

00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:53.390
There's data on
the actual servers.

00:03:53.390 --> 00:03:56.130
We run almost 10,000
servers around the world.

00:03:56.130 --> 00:03:57.650
And so what's going on there?

00:03:57.650 --> 00:04:00.810
And what's happening inside
of our customers' sites?

00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:05.270
So we process about 10
billion web requests a day.

00:04:05.270 --> 00:04:06.860
So just logging all
that, and sticking

00:04:06.860 --> 00:04:10.360
it somewhere, which, by the way,
is BigQuery, is already a feat.

00:04:10.360 --> 00:04:12.110
And there's all kinds
of interesting stuff

00:04:12.110 --> 00:04:13.908
in there about security--

00:04:13.908 --> 00:04:15.950
patterns you could look
at for security, patterns

00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:17.120
you could look at
for performance,

00:04:17.120 --> 00:04:19.700
like what could be cacheable or
blocked or something like this.

00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:21.690
So all kinds of interesting
stuff going on there--

00:04:21.690 --> 00:04:22.732
and that's true big data.

00:04:22.732 --> 00:04:25.190
Even at Google, 10
billion thingies a day

00:04:25.190 --> 00:04:27.490
is a lot, no matter who you are.

00:04:27.490 --> 00:04:29.490
So most of our data, of
course, is not big data.

00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:32.810
Most of anybody's data is not
big data, but it is just data,

00:04:32.810 --> 00:04:34.830
and some of it's big.

00:04:34.830 --> 00:04:36.860
So how do we go around
all these systems--

00:04:36.860 --> 00:04:40.310
the weblogs, the financial
systems, Salesforce,

00:04:40.310 --> 00:04:45.030
the support systems, tickets
and chats, our servers?

00:04:45.030 --> 00:04:47.390
How do we take all this
data and put it somewhere

00:04:47.390 --> 00:04:51.050
that is secure,
that we can clean,

00:04:51.050 --> 00:04:54.590
that we can audit,
that we can connect,

00:04:54.590 --> 00:04:56.720
that we can give to
other analysts and people

00:04:56.720 --> 00:04:58.940
in the company for
their various reasons

00:04:58.940 --> 00:05:00.680
why they care about data?

00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:01.940
How can we do that?

00:05:01.940 --> 00:05:04.130
That was our question.

00:05:04.130 --> 00:05:05.930
And so of course, I'm
going to describe how

00:05:05.930 --> 00:05:07.490
we did this this whole time.

00:05:07.490 --> 00:05:09.892
We called it "Grand Central
Data Station" because we're

00:05:09.892 --> 00:05:11.850
engineers, and we're
terrible at naming things,

00:05:11.850 --> 00:05:13.420
and so that's what we called it.

00:05:13.420 --> 00:05:14.780
Anyway, so this is what we did.

00:05:14.780 --> 00:05:18.470
And I'm going to show you how
we wrote some code around this,

00:05:18.470 --> 00:05:21.470
and what it got us, and also how
we leveraged tools from Google

00:05:21.470 --> 00:05:25.170
to do some of the hard
work for us as well.

00:05:25.170 --> 00:05:29.300
So the first thing is, when you
think about a big data project,

00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:32.780
is to define your
systems of record,

00:05:32.780 --> 00:05:34.050
which sounds simple enough.

00:05:34.050 --> 00:05:36.098
It's just, where all
the places there's data?

00:05:36.098 --> 00:05:37.640
But it's a little
bit more than that.

00:05:37.640 --> 00:05:39.720
It's where is their
data, and what data

00:05:39.720 --> 00:05:42.450
does that system actually own?

00:05:42.450 --> 00:05:45.620
So for example, Salesforce,
for us, owns the sales data.

00:05:45.620 --> 00:05:47.080
It's what the sales folks do.

00:05:49.970 --> 00:05:51.840
And we have customer
data in there.

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:56.210
But that's not the source
of truth for customers.

00:05:56.210 --> 00:05:57.920
That is not the
system that determines

00:05:57.920 --> 00:05:59.253
what is a customer or not.

00:05:59.253 --> 00:06:00.670
It determines what
the salesperson

00:06:00.670 --> 00:06:03.350
did, which isn't
necessarily the same thing.

00:06:03.350 --> 00:06:06.560
So we even have systems
that try to automate,

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:09.680
whenever there's a new customer,
putting it in Salesforce.

00:06:09.680 --> 00:06:12.740
We determined later that there
were 5,000 incorrect records

00:06:12.740 --> 00:06:13.880
there.

00:06:13.880 --> 00:06:16.052
Because there's a difference
between owning the data

00:06:16.052 --> 00:06:18.260
and having a copy of it or
having some kind of notion

00:06:18.260 --> 00:06:19.250
about it, right?

00:06:19.250 --> 00:06:20.020
So it's important.

00:06:20.020 --> 00:06:21.000
What are those things?

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:23.320
So for us, it's these
sorts of things,

00:06:23.320 --> 00:06:25.538
and what does it actually
own, and defining that

00:06:25.538 --> 00:06:26.330
and bringing it in.

00:06:26.330 --> 00:06:28.663
Because, ultimately, when you
put all the data together,

00:06:28.663 --> 00:06:31.520
of course, it's the
systems of records' data

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:33.195
that's the interesting part.

00:06:33.195 --> 00:06:34.820
And you might want
to push some of that

00:06:34.820 --> 00:06:36.560
back out, like we do
with customer data

00:06:36.560 --> 00:06:39.380
back to Salesforce, so that the
people who are using the tool

00:06:39.380 --> 00:06:40.500
have access to it.

00:06:40.500 --> 00:06:42.280
But it's important
to know what's what.

00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:45.775
And it's not obvious
right away what that is.

00:06:45.775 --> 00:06:47.900
So that's the first thing,
those systems of record.

00:06:47.900 --> 00:06:49.130
And then the second
thing is building

00:06:49.130 --> 00:06:50.755
a place where it can
all come together,

00:06:50.755 --> 00:06:52.480
so hence, the central station.

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:55.790
And again, there's all
these challenges with it.

00:06:55.790 --> 00:06:57.590
The places you're
pulling from each

00:06:57.590 --> 00:06:59.810
have its own way
of authenticating

00:06:59.810 --> 00:07:01.275
and how the credentials work.

00:07:01.275 --> 00:07:03.650
So there has to be a way to
securely store that and still

00:07:03.650 --> 00:07:05.837
pull it automatically,
which I'll describe.

00:07:05.837 --> 00:07:07.670
Of course, there are
different data formats.

00:07:07.670 --> 00:07:09.950
There's databases like
MySQL and Postgres,

00:07:09.950 --> 00:07:11.660
in our case, and
all these systems,

00:07:11.660 --> 00:07:13.610
like Salesforce,
Zendesk, et cetera,

00:07:13.610 --> 00:07:15.063
where it might be behind an API.

00:07:15.063 --> 00:07:17.480
So there are all these different
ways which it comes from.

00:07:17.480 --> 00:07:20.060
And by the way, the reason
to solve all this now

00:07:20.060 --> 00:07:22.678
is because this is why people
can't connect data today.

00:07:22.678 --> 00:07:24.470
Because it's too hard
to grab something out

00:07:24.470 --> 00:07:26.512
of Salesforce and something
out of something else

00:07:26.512 --> 00:07:28.320
and put it together
for any normal person.

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:30.790
So this is hard, but
it's kind of the point.

00:07:30.790 --> 00:07:33.442
Centralization is
important too because there

00:07:33.442 --> 00:07:35.900
are a lot of insights you can't
get unless you connect data

00:07:35.900 --> 00:07:37.460
from disparate sources.

00:07:37.460 --> 00:07:39.740
So you might want to say,
hey, we have customers

00:07:39.740 --> 00:07:41.630
where their traffic is growing.

00:07:41.630 --> 00:07:43.880
And they're using more
and more of our services.

00:07:43.880 --> 00:07:46.520
And they are either
not contacting support,

00:07:46.520 --> 00:07:48.140
or they have happy
support calls.

00:07:48.140 --> 00:07:49.520
In other words,
they're rating it high.

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:50.930
Here's a happy growing customer.

00:07:50.930 --> 00:07:53.305
That might be something a
salesperson would want to know,

00:07:53.305 --> 00:07:53.960
right.

00:07:53.960 --> 00:07:56.330
But that involves connecting
stuff from support

00:07:56.330 --> 00:07:57.470
and from the farm--

00:07:57.470 --> 00:08:00.110
which is what we
call our servers--

00:08:00.110 --> 00:08:00.750
and Salesforce.

00:08:00.750 --> 00:08:02.333
What if we're already
talking to them?

00:08:02.333 --> 00:08:04.980
Then they don't need to be
on a new contact report.

00:08:04.980 --> 00:08:07.272
So that's a very basic example.

00:08:07.272 --> 00:08:09.230
But it shows spanning a
whole bunch of systems.

00:08:09.230 --> 00:08:10.587
So centralization is important.

00:08:10.587 --> 00:08:12.920
And of course, the problem,
though, with centralization,

00:08:12.920 --> 00:08:14.780
is security.

00:08:14.780 --> 00:08:19.520
If I have everything in my
company in a table in BigQuery,

00:08:19.520 --> 00:08:21.970
whoever has access to that
has access to too much stuff,

00:08:21.970 --> 00:08:22.200
right?

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:24.158
It certainly violates
whatever kind of policies

00:08:24.158 --> 00:08:25.100
you might have.

00:08:25.100 --> 00:08:26.240
So it's a problem.

00:08:29.290 --> 00:08:32.360
So we centralize the data.

00:08:32.360 --> 00:08:34.309
When it's centralized,
and you have algorithms

00:08:34.309 --> 00:08:36.080
like cleaning stuff
up, it's really nice

00:08:36.080 --> 00:08:37.590
that everything's centralized
because you can run them

00:08:37.590 --> 00:08:38.429
on everything.

00:08:38.429 --> 00:08:40.380
For example, we
have country fields.

00:08:40.380 --> 00:08:43.280
And people use, sometimes it's
a two-digit code or three-digit

00:08:43.280 --> 00:08:45.440
code or fill-in-the-blank
like Salesforce.

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:47.930
And we have people in sales--

00:08:47.930 --> 00:08:50.600
I'm picking on sales a lot,
but you know, whatever.

00:08:50.600 --> 00:08:53.390
Our Salesforce has, like, 300
columns in the accounts table,

00:08:53.390 --> 00:08:54.930
so there's a lot to make fun of.

00:08:54.930 --> 00:08:57.252
Anyway, so for example,
in the country field,

00:08:57.252 --> 00:08:58.710
they would say,
like, London, which

00:08:58.710 --> 00:09:01.760
clearly was some
American who's confused

00:09:01.760 --> 00:09:04.020
about how countries work.

00:09:04.020 --> 00:09:05.355
And so there's all this stuff.

00:09:05.355 --> 00:09:06.980
And so having algorithms
that clean it,

00:09:06.980 --> 00:09:09.620
it's nice to make those,
test those, and apply those

00:09:09.620 --> 00:09:12.770
once, and not in all the
different systems, of course,

00:09:12.770 --> 00:09:15.530
analyzing, and connecting
the data, and so forth.

00:09:17.867 --> 00:09:20.450
And again, I'm going to show you
how we did that with our code

00:09:20.450 --> 00:09:22.640
and with BigQuery and
solved those problems.

00:09:22.640 --> 00:09:25.640
So we had a whole bunch of
goals, which we succeeded at.

00:09:25.640 --> 00:09:28.610
And some of them
are not obvious-- so

00:09:28.610 --> 00:09:31.490
automatically refreshing
the data, that's obvious.

00:09:31.490 --> 00:09:34.850
But let me skip down to an
audit trail where you can find,

00:09:34.850 --> 00:09:40.070
in an end user report, exactly
the whole trail of formulas

00:09:40.070 --> 00:09:41.870
of where it came
from, all the way back

00:09:41.870 --> 00:09:43.910
to the original
systems of record

00:09:43.910 --> 00:09:45.810
where the data really lives.

00:09:45.810 --> 00:09:47.840
That's something the
data analysts never get.

00:09:47.840 --> 00:09:49.715
Or they get documentation,
and it, of course,

00:09:49.715 --> 00:09:50.940
gets out of date right away.

00:09:50.940 --> 00:09:53.398
So that was going to be a really
powerful thing if we could

00:09:53.398 --> 00:09:55.400
do it, which I'll show you.

00:09:55.400 --> 00:09:58.850
Or what's Personally
Identifiable Information,

00:09:58.850 --> 00:09:59.570
or PII?

00:09:59.570 --> 00:10:03.110
There are a lot of privacy laws
around things that are PII.

00:10:03.110 --> 00:10:05.930
But if you're an end user with
a report, how do you know column

00:10:05.930 --> 00:10:08.870
three and seven are PII, and
you have to be extra careful,

00:10:08.870 --> 00:10:09.755
or they're not?

00:10:09.755 --> 00:10:11.380
Again, not something
you normally get--

00:10:11.380 --> 00:10:13.380
so there's some neat stuff
I'm going to show you

00:10:13.380 --> 00:10:15.510
that we are able to achieve.

00:10:15.510 --> 00:10:17.547
So we picked
BigQuery, of course,

00:10:17.547 --> 00:10:18.630
because I'm standing here.

00:10:18.630 --> 00:10:21.063
So obviously, we
use Google stuff.

00:10:21.063 --> 00:10:22.230
There's a couple of reasons.

00:10:22.230 --> 00:10:26.210
One is, as a company
that has 10,000 servers,

00:10:26.210 --> 00:10:29.060
we know for sure we do
not want more servers.

00:10:29.060 --> 00:10:31.180
We know that stinks, and
we don't want servers.

00:10:31.180 --> 00:10:34.160
And a lot of these databases,
even when they're managed,

00:10:34.160 --> 00:10:36.673
and, in fact, even other
databases at Google,

00:10:36.673 --> 00:10:38.090
even though they're
managed, there

00:10:38.090 --> 00:10:39.200
are [? knobs ?]
and things still.

00:10:39.200 --> 00:10:41.360
It's like, well, do you want
to start with three databases

00:10:41.360 --> 00:10:43.040
all over, because
high availability.

00:10:43.040 --> 00:10:44.510
and here's how you
do the failover.

00:10:44.510 --> 00:10:45.630
And here's how you might scale.

00:10:45.630 --> 00:10:46.640
And then you've got
to redo the disc.

00:10:46.640 --> 00:10:48.920
And you're just, like, that's
not what I want to do ever.

00:10:48.920 --> 00:10:49.920
Nobody wants to do that.

00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:50.980
Do it for me.

00:10:50.980 --> 00:10:52.730
And one of the great
things about BigQuery

00:10:52.730 --> 00:10:53.690
is it really is that.

00:10:53.690 --> 00:10:56.330
It really is-- today I
have one row is in a table.

00:10:56.330 --> 00:10:58.820
And tomorrow, I have 1
trillion rows in a table.

00:10:58.820 --> 00:11:00.800
And I just pay a little
bit more for storage.

00:11:00.800 --> 00:11:03.680
That's actually how it
works, which is really nice.

00:11:03.680 --> 00:11:06.350
Again, we have these
huge amounts of weblogs.

00:11:06.350 --> 00:11:09.740
So that alone is useful to
have something that scales.

00:11:09.740 --> 00:11:13.110
Performance in BigQuery
is interesting.

00:11:13.110 --> 00:11:15.800
We can run a query over
these logs and process

00:11:15.800 --> 00:11:18.860
trillions of rows, summarize
all kinds of stuff,

00:11:18.860 --> 00:11:22.160
and get an interesting
answer in seven seconds.

00:11:22.160 --> 00:11:24.390
Also, you can ask it what
time it is in a query,

00:11:24.390 --> 00:11:26.250
and it will also
take seven seconds.

00:11:26.250 --> 00:11:28.960
So everything in BigQuery
takes a little bit of time,

00:11:28.960 --> 00:11:30.915
but you can ask it
as much as you want.

00:11:30.915 --> 00:11:32.040
That's not completely true.

00:11:32.040 --> 00:11:34.457
If you stack up queries, or
you really have a lot of data,

00:11:34.457 --> 00:11:35.630
it could start slowing down.

00:11:35.630 --> 00:11:37.470
I'll show you something
that we did about that.

00:11:37.470 --> 00:11:39.845
But in general, the performance
for large amounts of data

00:11:39.845 --> 00:11:41.210
is really nice.

00:11:41.210 --> 00:11:43.490
One area that's not nice
is, if you have a report,

00:11:43.490 --> 00:11:45.230
and you're filtering
stuff, and changing stuff,

00:11:45.230 --> 00:11:46.820
and each one of those
is a different query.

00:11:46.820 --> 00:11:48.362
Because in those
seven seconds- makes

00:11:48.362 --> 00:11:50.190
the iteration time really slow.

00:11:50.190 --> 00:11:52.820
So that's where using some kind
of BI tool in front of BigQuery

00:11:52.820 --> 00:11:54.170
is useful.

00:11:54.170 --> 00:11:55.780
And so in our case,
we use Looker.

00:11:55.780 --> 00:11:57.800
But of course, there's
a lot of good ones.

00:11:57.800 --> 00:12:00.110
That's just what we
happened to pick.

00:12:00.110 --> 00:12:03.260
And what we found is that kills
that, sort of-- you might say,

00:12:03.260 --> 00:12:05.880
the equivalent of the
last-mile problem of,

00:12:05.880 --> 00:12:07.380
I'm an end user
looking at a report,

00:12:07.380 --> 00:12:09.110
and I want to quickly
look at stuff.

00:12:09.110 --> 00:12:12.170
If you have a BI tool there,
it can do that last-mile set

00:12:12.170 --> 00:12:13.280
of filtering and stuff.

00:12:13.280 --> 00:12:16.730
And that can be super fast and
convenient for that person.

00:12:16.730 --> 00:12:19.310
And you don't incur the
problems of BigQuery's slowness.

00:12:22.145 --> 00:12:23.270
So security is another one.

00:12:23.270 --> 00:12:24.940
So I mentioned
this thing where--

00:12:24.940 --> 00:12:26.802
I mentioned the security
problem of, OK, I

00:12:26.802 --> 00:12:28.010
put all my data in one place.

00:12:28.010 --> 00:12:31.250
And data people call
that a data lake, right?

00:12:31.250 --> 00:12:33.170
I don't know if that's
a good analogy, a lake.

00:12:33.170 --> 00:12:34.445
It's kind of a good
analogy in that it's

00:12:34.445 --> 00:12:36.330
just a whole bunch of
stuff all mixed up,

00:12:36.330 --> 00:12:37.580
and you can't find it anymore.

00:12:37.580 --> 00:12:40.050
And it's all there, I
guess, but who knows?

00:12:40.050 --> 00:12:41.600
So I guess it's an OK analogy.

00:12:41.600 --> 00:12:44.483
But anyway, when you put
all the data together,

00:12:44.483 --> 00:12:46.400
the good news is, you
can clean it and connect

00:12:46.400 --> 00:12:47.317
it and all that stuff.

00:12:47.317 --> 00:12:49.610
And the bad news
is, you kind of want

00:12:49.610 --> 00:12:51.210
no one to have access to it.

00:12:51.210 --> 00:12:53.487
So it's kind of a problem.

00:12:53.487 --> 00:12:55.070
So here's what you
can do in BigQuery,

00:12:55.070 --> 00:12:56.910
and this is what
we've been doing.

00:12:56.910 --> 00:12:58.327
So you put all the
data somewhere.

00:12:58.327 --> 00:12:59.785
Obviously, this is
all locked down,

00:12:59.785 --> 00:13:01.180
and almost no one can get to it.

00:13:01.180 --> 00:13:03.272
Maybe service accounts
can and so forth.

00:13:03.272 --> 00:13:04.730
Then the marketing
department needs

00:13:04.730 --> 00:13:06.590
some stuff, so what do we do?

00:13:06.590 --> 00:13:08.510
So we create what is,
in BigQuery, called

00:13:08.510 --> 00:13:11.140
a dataset, which is
really just like a folder.

00:13:11.140 --> 00:13:13.860
And you can put stuff in the
folder, like views and tables.

00:13:13.860 --> 00:13:15.910
So in particular, we
put views in there.

00:13:15.910 --> 00:13:19.700
So the way it works is, we
figure, oh, marketing-- there's

00:13:19.700 --> 00:13:22.200
some funnel report or
something that they need.

00:13:22.200 --> 00:13:25.550
So we can pick all this data
out and make some awesome view

00:13:25.550 --> 00:13:28.175
that contains all the data one
would need for this report.

00:13:28.175 --> 00:13:32.270
So we take that view, and we
put it in a marketing dataset.

00:13:32.270 --> 00:13:35.030
Then we give
marketing, the group,

00:13:35.030 --> 00:13:37.470
access to that
marketing dataset.

00:13:37.470 --> 00:13:39.620
And now, although marketing
does not have access

00:13:39.620 --> 00:13:42.110
to the underlying tables,
cannot go in there,

00:13:42.110 --> 00:13:45.190
they are allowed to
see what's in the view.

00:13:45.190 --> 00:13:48.680
So that means really easily--
like point-and-click easy--

00:13:48.680 --> 00:13:52.030
we can provide datasets
in the form of views

00:13:52.030 --> 00:13:55.490
to whomever, have complete
access control there,

00:13:55.490 --> 00:13:58.330
and any policies we want
about what we show to whom

00:13:58.330 --> 00:14:00.700
and how we check that off,
or not having much policies,

00:14:00.700 --> 00:14:01.825
depending on the situation.

00:14:01.825 --> 00:14:04.720
It's very easy to
be flexible there.

00:14:04.720 --> 00:14:06.850
And so we can have
the data together,

00:14:06.850 --> 00:14:09.457
create those unique
reports, but then

00:14:09.457 --> 00:14:11.290
give people only the
resulting report, which

00:14:11.290 --> 00:14:12.310
they can then take directly.

00:14:12.310 --> 00:14:13.330
They can take it to Data Studio.

00:14:13.330 --> 00:14:14.913
They can take it to
Looker, et cetera,

00:14:14.913 --> 00:14:16.880
however it is that they
want to consume it.

00:14:16.880 --> 00:14:18.945
So the security
model in BigQuery

00:14:18.945 --> 00:14:21.070
that allows us to do that
and use the normal Google

00:14:21.070 --> 00:14:23.740
users to do that was
extremely convenient

00:14:23.740 --> 00:14:26.340
for us to just be able to make
these things easily and not

00:14:26.340 --> 00:14:27.590
worry too much about security.

00:14:27.590 --> 00:14:30.935
So that was really helpful.

00:14:30.935 --> 00:14:32.560
So this is roughly
what we built. So we

00:14:32.560 --> 00:14:35.380
built this system in Python.

00:14:35.380 --> 00:14:37.930
And the part that's
filled in a little bit

00:14:37.930 --> 00:14:39.850
is the part that's
our Python code.

00:14:39.850 --> 00:14:41.225
And, again, I'm
going to show you

00:14:41.225 --> 00:14:43.240
a little bit about what we did.

00:14:43.240 --> 00:14:44.515
And so the main thing here--

00:14:44.515 --> 00:14:46.223
because much of this
won't be surprising.

00:14:46.223 --> 00:14:48.580
It's just boxes and arrows
with words in the middle

00:14:48.580 --> 00:14:51.297
that you've seen before,
mostly, so it's not a big deal.

00:14:51.297 --> 00:14:52.630
But here's the interesting part.

00:14:52.630 --> 00:14:55.090
You see, in the very, very
front there, in the data engine,

00:14:55.090 --> 00:14:57.100
it says specifications and JSON.

00:14:57.100 --> 00:14:59.235
I'm going to describe this
in detail and show you.

00:14:59.235 --> 00:15:01.360
This is kind of the key
that makes this whole thing

00:15:01.360 --> 00:15:01.918
work well.

00:15:01.918 --> 00:15:03.460
And If we didn't do
this, and we just

00:15:03.460 --> 00:15:05.530
tried to use BigQuery
alone or some BI tool,

00:15:05.530 --> 00:15:07.900
we wouldn't be able to
achieve all those bullet

00:15:07.900 --> 00:15:08.960
points on the other page.

00:15:08.960 --> 00:15:10.627
So I'm going to explain
how he did that.

00:15:10.627 --> 00:15:14.750
But roughly, we have JSON
specifications that say,

00:15:14.750 --> 00:15:17.410
here's how to pull data
from systems of record

00:15:17.410 --> 00:15:19.580
and also JSON
specifications that say,

00:15:19.580 --> 00:15:23.940
here's how to transform that
data into more usable views.

00:15:23.940 --> 00:15:27.040
I'm going to show you that.

00:15:27.040 --> 00:15:29.140
Now, these are our data
sources, CMDB, BAPI.

00:15:29.140 --> 00:15:30.460
These are our own names.

00:15:30.460 --> 00:15:32.630
There are own internal tools.

00:15:32.630 --> 00:15:34.960
So we're reaching into things
like MySQL and Postgres

00:15:34.960 --> 00:15:38.920
to grab stuff out of there and
pulling that into BigQuery.

00:15:38.920 --> 00:15:43.240
And also we used segment.io
for some of these SaaS tools

00:15:43.240 --> 00:15:46.043
that have APIs, like Salesforce
and Zendesk and Stripe.

00:15:46.043 --> 00:15:47.710
And I know today, it
was just announced,

00:15:47.710 --> 00:15:50.030
there are even more tools
now to automate that stuff.

00:15:50.030 --> 00:15:50.530
So, hooray!

00:15:50.530 --> 00:15:51.910
Maybe this is even easier.

00:15:51.910 --> 00:15:54.250
But anyway, when we
were doing this just,

00:15:54.250 --> 00:15:57.800
maybe four months ago, Segment
was able to handle that,

00:15:57.800 --> 00:15:59.677
and we had to handle
our own stuff.

00:15:59.677 --> 00:16:01.510
Now, I think it's even
easier because Google

00:16:01.510 --> 00:16:03.370
wants you to put a lot of
data in BigQuery, of course.

00:16:03.370 --> 00:16:05.000
Because then you're
paying them more money

00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:05.590
and using their stuff.

00:16:05.590 --> 00:16:06.740
Of course, they
want you to do that.

00:16:06.740 --> 00:16:08.710
Of course, it's going to
get more easy to pull stuff

00:16:08.710 --> 00:16:09.293
into BigQuery.

00:16:09.293 --> 00:16:10.417
But that's good.

00:16:10.417 --> 00:16:12.250
So anyway, once it's
in there, then the data

00:16:12.250 --> 00:16:13.090
gets transformed--

00:16:13.090 --> 00:16:14.257
I'm going to show you that--

00:16:14.257 --> 00:16:15.910
into those reports,
like I mentioned,

00:16:15.910 --> 00:16:18.610
and various documentation,
and so forth.

00:16:18.610 --> 00:16:19.270
OK.

00:16:19.270 --> 00:16:23.350
So here's an example
of fraction of JSON.

00:16:23.350 --> 00:16:25.090
I hope you can read that.

00:16:25.090 --> 00:16:26.650
If you can't, then
it's green text

00:16:26.650 --> 00:16:31.168
that looks like JSON that
is about systems of record.

00:16:31.168 --> 00:16:33.460
So the way it works is, we
say, all right, well, here's

00:16:33.460 --> 00:16:35.950
a table somewhere.

00:16:35.950 --> 00:16:38.937
Here's a key to a
credentials file,

00:16:38.937 --> 00:16:41.020
which, of course, is
elsewhere and not checked in,

00:16:41.020 --> 00:16:44.390
as you would expect, for
how to get to this system.

00:16:44.390 --> 00:16:47.740
And then we define all the
columns with lots of metadata.

00:16:47.740 --> 00:16:48.880
What's the data type?

00:16:48.880 --> 00:16:51.130
Is it null or not?

00:16:51.130 --> 00:16:56.200
Is this a primary key for the
table, a human description,

00:16:56.200 --> 00:17:02.080
like in just regular prose, of
what this is, and also possibly

00:17:02.080 --> 00:17:04.780
some metadata like, what is
the quality of this data?

00:17:04.780 --> 00:17:06.640
Is this PII, some
of those things

00:17:06.640 --> 00:17:07.849
that I mentioned earlier?

00:17:07.849 --> 00:17:09.880
We can tag them in here
just by saying it's true

00:17:09.880 --> 00:17:11.810
or giving it a number.

00:17:11.810 --> 00:17:13.660
So by doing this,
already what we

00:17:13.660 --> 00:17:16.960
can do is automate the
sucking of the data out

00:17:16.960 --> 00:17:20.109
of the systems of record and
stuffing them into BigQuery.

00:17:20.109 --> 00:17:24.405
So what we do is, we
say, OK, every time we

00:17:24.405 --> 00:17:25.780
retrieve the data,
we want to get

00:17:25.780 --> 00:17:28.089
the data we haven't seen yet.

00:17:28.089 --> 00:17:29.950
So we do that by looking
at the primary keys

00:17:29.950 --> 00:17:34.777
and saying, well, whatever we've
gotten up to whatever date,

00:17:34.777 --> 00:17:36.610
anything that's newer
than that, we'll take.

00:17:36.610 --> 00:17:38.980
As well as if the data's
equal, then whatever

00:17:38.980 --> 00:17:42.490
is newer than the last primary
key on that date that we got.

00:17:42.490 --> 00:17:45.010
So in other words, tie
goes to the primary key.

00:17:45.010 --> 00:17:46.630
So by doing that,
we can incrementally

00:17:46.630 --> 00:17:47.800
update as often as we want.

00:17:47.800 --> 00:17:49.980
At the moment, it's every hour.

00:17:49.980 --> 00:17:52.362
But it's easy to do that.

00:17:52.362 --> 00:17:54.320
The other thing we can
do is validate the data.

00:17:54.320 --> 00:17:56.720
So if we say, in
here, it can be null.

00:17:56.720 --> 00:17:57.220
Fine.

00:17:57.220 --> 00:17:59.590
But if we say in here,
the data cannot be null,

00:17:59.590 --> 00:18:02.005
but we see one come
through, we can flag it.

00:18:02.005 --> 00:18:03.130
We can decide not to do it.

00:18:03.130 --> 00:18:04.690
We can stall.

00:18:04.690 --> 00:18:06.875
We can let it in any
way and emit a warning.

00:18:06.875 --> 00:18:09.250
Whatever is the way that you
want to handle that is fine.

00:18:09.250 --> 00:18:12.760
But we're able to check and
validate the data on the way

00:18:12.760 --> 00:18:17.110
in from the system of
record, which might be dirty.

00:18:17.110 --> 00:18:19.940
There are more things that this
can do, which you'll see next.

00:18:19.940 --> 00:18:21.940
So this is an example of
JSON, which looks just

00:18:21.940 --> 00:18:24.910
like the other JSON,
if you can't see it,

00:18:24.910 --> 00:18:26.980
which is a transformation.

00:18:26.980 --> 00:18:30.670
And here, the JSON has
got structures which

00:18:30.670 --> 00:18:32.658
look a lot like a SQL query.

00:18:32.658 --> 00:18:34.825
So there's a SOURCES in
there, which is like a JOIN.

00:18:34.825 --> 00:18:37.600
It says, here are the different
tables I want to pick from,

00:18:37.600 --> 00:18:40.480
maybe some join
keys, some columns

00:18:40.480 --> 00:18:42.837
I may want to inherit
from that table.

00:18:42.837 --> 00:18:44.920
And also, here are some
columns I want to compute.

00:18:44.920 --> 00:18:47.380
In this one, there's
one called is_fraud.

00:18:47.380 --> 00:18:48.850
And there's an
expression in there,

00:18:48.850 --> 00:18:50.350
which, if you look
at it, looks sort

00:18:50.350 --> 00:18:53.620
of like Lisp or something--

00:18:53.620 --> 00:18:56.320
function, argument, argument,
argument, maybe the arguments

00:18:56.320 --> 00:18:58.420
have functions too,
like Lisp, right?

00:18:58.420 --> 00:19:00.310
In other words,
like a parsed tree--

00:19:00.310 --> 00:19:02.420
that's what it looks like.

00:19:02.420 --> 00:19:06.310
So here, we're checking whether
some possibly null field

00:19:06.310 --> 00:19:09.130
in a fraud table is either the
word fraud or the word band.

00:19:09.130 --> 00:19:11.848
And if so, then that means it's
a Boolean, IsFraud and blah,

00:19:11.848 --> 00:19:13.390
blah, blah-- this
kind of basic stuff

00:19:13.390 --> 00:19:16.050
that you do when you clean
data or calculate stuff.

00:19:16.050 --> 00:19:18.550
There are also filters, which
is sort of like a WHERE clause

00:19:18.550 --> 00:19:22.173
in SQL, right-- only the rows
which match this and so forth.

00:19:22.173 --> 00:19:24.340
And all these expressions
are sitting there in JSON.

00:19:24.340 --> 00:19:26.632
And so, of course, you might
think, well, this is dumb.

00:19:26.632 --> 00:19:27.370
Just write SQL.

00:19:27.370 --> 00:19:29.210
Like, this is a
lot more verbose.

00:19:29.210 --> 00:19:30.292
What are you doing?

00:19:30.292 --> 00:19:31.750
And so what we're
doing is actually

00:19:31.750 --> 00:19:34.370
getting a whole lot of neat
benefits by doing this.

00:19:34.370 --> 00:19:36.550
So one of the things we can
do with this Python that

00:19:36.550 --> 00:19:38.758
reads in the JSON, of course,
one of things it can do

00:19:38.758 --> 00:19:41.442
is make the SQL that makes
this view in BigQuery

00:19:41.442 --> 00:19:43.525
and stick it in BigQuery,
which, of course, we do.

00:19:43.525 --> 00:19:45.370
So duh.

00:19:45.370 --> 00:19:48.280
Other things it can do
is check our own errors.

00:19:48.280 --> 00:19:49.810
Are the data types right?

00:19:49.810 --> 00:19:51.850
Are we handling nulls correctly?

00:19:51.850 --> 00:19:54.370
So all these kinds of
things that can be wrong,

00:19:54.370 --> 00:19:57.820
this code can detect it, first
off, so that we don't just

00:19:57.820 --> 00:19:59.170
accidentally find it later.

00:19:59.170 --> 00:20:00.700
Sometimes databases
will complain.

00:20:00.700 --> 00:20:03.477
Sometimes they won't and
just sort of proceed.

00:20:03.477 --> 00:20:04.310
And that's not good.

00:20:04.310 --> 00:20:05.390
We want to catch--

00:20:05.390 --> 00:20:07.510
we want to be extra
pedantic here.

00:20:07.510 --> 00:20:11.380
We want this to be like
extra linted, extra careful

00:20:11.380 --> 00:20:13.515
here because data
is so important.

00:20:13.515 --> 00:20:14.890
It is important
to get everything

00:20:14.890 --> 00:20:16.730
exactly right in this use case.

00:20:16.730 --> 00:20:20.380
So we don't want to
be sloppy at all.

00:20:20.380 --> 00:20:22.690
Or things that are convenient--
so for example, maybe

00:20:22.690 --> 00:20:24.810
I'm concatenating
a string together.

00:20:24.810 --> 00:20:27.970
And in SQL, all those things
have to be strings already.

00:20:27.970 --> 00:20:30.560
But we can say, oh, look,
you're concatenating strings.

00:20:30.560 --> 00:20:31.810
Oh, look, that one's a number.

00:20:31.810 --> 00:20:32.860
Eh, it's cool.

00:20:32.860 --> 00:20:34.720
We'll just throw the
CAST operator in there

00:20:34.720 --> 00:20:36.097
for you when we make the SQL.

00:20:36.097 --> 00:20:37.180
You don't need to do that.

00:20:37.180 --> 00:20:38.823
That's just a bother.

00:20:38.823 --> 00:20:40.240
So there are various
things in SQL

00:20:40.240 --> 00:20:42.490
which can be annoying,
which we can actually

00:20:42.490 --> 00:20:44.098
just clean up and hide.

00:20:44.098 --> 00:20:45.140
I'll give you an example.

00:20:45.140 --> 00:20:47.550
There is a really
fancy way in BigQuery

00:20:47.550 --> 00:20:51.350
where you can refer to things
like the previous row, which

00:20:51.350 --> 00:20:52.350
is kind of weird, right?

00:20:52.350 --> 00:20:53.560
You're emitting
things, and you can

00:20:53.560 --> 00:20:55.643
ask about the previous--
or even the next one that

00:20:55.643 --> 00:20:57.240
hasn't been emitted yet.

00:20:57.240 --> 00:20:58.100
That's pretty cool.

00:20:58.100 --> 00:21:00.475
Because there are all kinds
of really neat calculations--

00:21:00.475 --> 00:21:02.670
running sums, or numbering
things a certain way--

00:21:02.670 --> 00:21:05.670
all kinds of neat things you
can do if you can reach forward

00:21:05.670 --> 00:21:07.500
and backward a little
bit and do stuff.

00:21:07.500 --> 00:21:11.160
You can do that in BigQuery, but
the expressions are confusing.

00:21:11.160 --> 00:21:14.580
They can be kind of
long just to do that.

00:21:14.580 --> 00:21:17.790
And especially someone
who understands enough

00:21:17.790 --> 00:21:20.620
SQL to want to do that and
write this kind of thing,

00:21:20.620 --> 00:21:22.920
but doesn't understand enough
to get into the windowing

00:21:22.920 --> 00:21:24.780
systems and analytic
functions in BigQuery,

00:21:24.780 --> 00:21:26.500
which are confusing--

00:21:26.500 --> 00:21:30.900
so we can provide a function
called, like, NEXT or PREVIOUS.

00:21:30.900 --> 00:21:33.750
And you just say I want
the next field of this.

00:21:33.750 --> 00:21:37.010
And it turns into this
BigQuery expression.

00:21:37.010 --> 00:21:39.010
And thanks, BigQuery, for
being able to do that.

00:21:39.010 --> 00:21:40.410
That's cool.

00:21:40.410 --> 00:21:42.900
But thanks to this,
the operator does not

00:21:42.900 --> 00:21:44.490
have to know how to
do that correctly.

00:21:44.490 --> 00:21:47.400
We can generate that confusing
thing for the operator.

00:21:47.400 --> 00:21:50.740
So that's nice, because we can
do relatively complex things

00:21:50.740 --> 00:21:54.120
that don't look so complex
when you're writing it.

00:21:54.120 --> 00:21:56.680
So even just making the SQL,
there are advantages here.

00:21:56.680 --> 00:21:59.640
The next thing,
though, is what else

00:21:59.640 --> 00:22:03.340
can we do now that we have
all this metadata about it?

00:22:03.340 --> 00:22:07.530
So one of the things
that kind of--

00:22:07.530 --> 00:22:11.740
classically annoying about
data is documentation.

00:22:11.740 --> 00:22:14.370
You've got to have good docs
because the end users are not

00:22:14.370 --> 00:22:15.697
programmers, generally.

00:22:15.697 --> 00:22:17.280
They could be analysts
or even someone

00:22:17.280 --> 00:22:20.250
just looking at a report
who's not even an analyst.

00:22:20.250 --> 00:22:22.650
And you want to document stuff
like how this came to be,

00:22:22.650 --> 00:22:25.860
or what these fields mean,
or allow someone to audit it,

00:22:25.860 --> 00:22:27.383
especially in finance.

00:22:27.383 --> 00:22:28.050
You have audits.

00:22:28.050 --> 00:22:30.120
And so you need you need
that traceability of where

00:22:30.120 --> 00:22:32.537
this data comes from, especially
if you're going to use it

00:22:32.537 --> 00:22:34.850
in a financial context,
and your company

00:22:34.850 --> 00:22:35.850
gets to a certain scale.

00:22:35.850 --> 00:22:37.800
You must be able
to have an audit

00:22:37.800 --> 00:22:39.187
or trace of where
that came from.

00:22:39.187 --> 00:22:40.270
So you have documentation.

00:22:40.270 --> 00:22:42.660
We know how that goes-- code and
documentation being separate.

00:22:42.660 --> 00:22:43.860
That means they never
match, and then you

00:22:43.860 --> 00:22:44.985
don't know what's going on.

00:22:44.985 --> 00:22:47.700
Of course, that's how
it works, but not here.

00:22:47.700 --> 00:22:49.380
Because our documentation
is completely

00:22:49.380 --> 00:22:51.750
generated only
from all that JSON.

00:22:51.750 --> 00:22:54.575
And so the documentation and
the queries are never different.

00:22:54.575 --> 00:22:55.950
So here's an
example of something

00:22:55.950 --> 00:22:58.380
that our documentation creates.

00:22:58.380 --> 00:23:02.020
So this is a tree of how
a certain table, called

00:23:02.020 --> 00:23:05.130
the ledger, which is used
by finance, gets created.

00:23:05.130 --> 00:23:06.990
And so you can see
here, in purple

00:23:06.990 --> 00:23:08.880
are the systems
of record tables,

00:23:08.880 --> 00:23:12.030
and in yellow are
these transformations,

00:23:12.030 --> 00:23:13.560
and then, finally, the table.

00:23:13.560 --> 00:23:15.870
Now, of course, there's
individual documentation

00:23:15.870 --> 00:23:17.700
for every one of these steps.

00:23:17.700 --> 00:23:20.450
But right away, you can
see, as an end user,

00:23:20.450 --> 00:23:21.780
like, oh, this is complicated.

00:23:21.780 --> 00:23:23.700
And you get a sense of
what goes into what.

00:23:23.700 --> 00:23:25.170
And so if you are
auditing, if you

00:23:25.170 --> 00:23:26.940
are trying to figure it out,
you have a map, at least,

00:23:26.940 --> 00:23:28.310
of this very complicated thing.

00:23:28.310 --> 00:23:30.430
You at least have a
map of what's going on,

00:23:30.430 --> 00:23:32.003
which is already helpful.

00:23:32.003 --> 00:23:33.420
This, by the way,
is created using

00:23:33.420 --> 00:23:34.650
something called Graphviz.

00:23:34.650 --> 00:23:36.150
And if you haven't
seen it, it's one

00:23:36.150 --> 00:23:37.983
of these tools that
are, like, 20 years old,

00:23:37.983 --> 00:23:39.772
but most people still
don't know about it.

00:23:39.772 --> 00:23:40.480
It's really cool.

00:23:40.480 --> 00:23:43.020
You can just say things like,
this node goes into that one,

00:23:43.020 --> 00:23:45.790
and this one goes into that one,
and it lays it out like this.

00:23:45.790 --> 00:23:47.100
And then you can say,
oh, and also this one

00:23:47.100 --> 00:23:48.360
has a yellow background.

00:23:48.360 --> 00:23:49.823
So actually, this is real easy.

00:23:49.823 --> 00:23:51.240
You can do it on
the command line.

00:23:51.240 --> 00:23:53.640
This is with a Python
client wrapper.

00:23:53.640 --> 00:23:56.280
So this whole thing
just takes something

00:23:56.280 --> 00:23:58.530
like 10 lines of code total.

00:23:58.530 --> 00:24:00.270
Because we just look
through the JSON.

00:24:00.270 --> 00:24:01.980
And how do we tell
the dependencies?

00:24:01.980 --> 00:24:03.395
Because we have the joins.

00:24:03.395 --> 00:24:05.520
And since we have the joins,
we can just walk them,

00:24:05.520 --> 00:24:06.810
and that's the dependencies.

00:24:06.810 --> 00:24:08.590
It's actually pretty easy.

00:24:08.590 --> 00:24:10.410
And then we feed it
into this Graphviz

00:24:10.410 --> 00:24:12.520
and just give it arrows.

00:24:12.520 --> 00:24:15.810
And so this is almost no
code to crap out this ping.

00:24:15.810 --> 00:24:17.700
And so we do this on
every single table.

00:24:17.700 --> 00:24:21.300
Automatically, we
have this nice map.

00:24:21.300 --> 00:24:30.680
Another example-- so we have
hyperlinked HTML documentation

00:24:30.680 --> 00:24:32.990
on every single column
and every single table

00:24:32.990 --> 00:24:35.195
in the whole system,
which would really

00:24:35.195 --> 00:24:36.320
suck to have to do by hand.

00:24:36.320 --> 00:24:38.120
I don't know many tech
writers you'd need

00:24:38.120 --> 00:24:39.787
and what you'd have
to bribe them for it

00:24:39.787 --> 00:24:41.720
to just sit through
it. but anyway--

00:24:41.720 --> 00:24:45.450
so this is just two examples
of what this looks like.

00:24:45.450 --> 00:24:47.760
So if you look
here on the left--

00:24:47.760 --> 00:24:50.870
I know it's going to be a
little bit hard to read.

00:24:50.870 --> 00:24:53.987
What we're doing is saying quite
a few things about the data.

00:24:53.987 --> 00:24:55.820
So I'm going to walk
you through some of it.

00:24:55.820 --> 00:24:59.030
So if you look on the
left, the left column

00:24:59.030 --> 00:25:01.280
where it says
agency_natural_sales_team,

00:25:01.280 --> 00:25:04.940
what we're doing here is
saying, hey, for a given agency

00:25:04.940 --> 00:25:07.970
that we work with, there's
some sales team where,

00:25:07.970 --> 00:25:10.190
because of their
geographic location,

00:25:10.190 --> 00:25:12.680
they're the natural sales team
to have this agency, which

00:25:12.680 --> 00:25:14.780
may not be the same
thing as the sales team

00:25:14.780 --> 00:25:16.250
that actually has the agency.

00:25:16.250 --> 00:25:19.310
This is the detailed data stuff
that's actually really helpful.

00:25:19.310 --> 00:25:22.050
Without a complex system, it's
hard to get this kind of data.

00:25:22.050 --> 00:25:24.170
So it says that it's
not null over there

00:25:24.170 --> 00:25:26.420
in the corner, which is nice
if you're making reports.

00:25:26.420 --> 00:25:28.580
And it says data quality is 70%.

00:25:28.580 --> 00:25:30.540
Where did that come from?

00:25:30.540 --> 00:25:33.200
So again, we can
annotate fields and say

00:25:33.200 --> 00:25:34.790
what the data
quality is all back

00:25:34.790 --> 00:25:37.790
in the system of record area.

00:25:37.790 --> 00:25:39.320
So then in the
transformation JSON,

00:25:39.320 --> 00:25:40.980
you can imagine
saying, all right,

00:25:40.980 --> 00:25:43.552
if we know that you picked
this expression, and this one,

00:25:43.552 --> 00:25:46.010
and this one, and this one,
and added them, and one of them

00:25:46.010 --> 00:25:48.530
has a lower data
quality, then the result

00:25:48.530 --> 00:25:49.700
has a lower data quality.

00:25:49.700 --> 00:25:52.010
And of course, you could
argue about, if two of them

00:25:52.010 --> 00:25:54.770
have low, do they multiply,
or just the lowest?

00:25:54.770 --> 00:25:58.010
You could argue about how data
quality should be measured

00:25:58.010 --> 00:26:00.050
and how that should
progress, whatever?

00:26:00.050 --> 00:26:02.635
Point is, you could pick
and decide how that works.

00:26:02.635 --> 00:26:05.010
And so, in the end result, we
can see it's not that good.

00:26:05.010 --> 00:26:07.130
And the reason it's not
that good is this data

00:26:07.130 --> 00:26:10.070
comes from that thing I
told you about earlier where

00:26:10.070 --> 00:26:11.300
people put in strange data.

00:26:11.300 --> 00:26:13.460
And so this is
OK, but not great.

00:26:13.460 --> 00:26:16.700
Also, we imported data from
a data source that we bought.

00:26:16.700 --> 00:26:19.460
And we know that data
source is about 70% right.

00:26:19.460 --> 00:26:22.680
We were able to write all that
in in the original data sources

00:26:22.680 --> 00:26:24.650
so that, later, when
someone's computing

00:26:24.650 --> 00:26:27.890
this thing, this fairly
complicated thing, that

00:26:27.890 --> 00:26:29.140
just shows up.

00:26:29.140 --> 00:26:31.820
And again, a human doesn't
have to maintain that.

00:26:31.820 --> 00:26:35.070
It just shows up, and
it's in the documentation.

00:26:35.070 --> 00:26:37.077
So the next thing it does is--

00:26:37.077 --> 00:26:38.660
again, there are
people who write SQL,

00:26:38.660 --> 00:26:40.593
and that's probably the
people in this room.

00:26:40.593 --> 00:26:42.260
And then, for everybody
that writes SQL,

00:26:42.260 --> 00:26:44.970
there are 10 or maybe
even 50 other people

00:26:44.970 --> 00:26:47.930
at the company who need to
consume it and don't know SQL.

00:26:47.930 --> 00:26:50.420
So the other thing we
do is we write out,

00:26:50.420 --> 00:26:54.795
in kind of English-ish,
what is the formula?

00:26:54.795 --> 00:26:57.170
Which, if you think about it,
if you have the expression,

00:26:57.170 --> 00:26:59.550
it's not that hard just
to format that thing

00:26:59.550 --> 00:27:02.460
as HTML instead of
formatting it as SQL.

00:27:02.460 --> 00:27:05.640
So in this case, if
this condition holds,

00:27:05.640 --> 00:27:08.990
then we use this value.

00:27:08.990 --> 00:27:11.550
This is a SWITCH
statement, right?

00:27:11.550 --> 00:27:13.700
OK, so the thing on the
left-- if the first value

00:27:13.700 --> 00:27:17.360
matches any of these,
so that's an IN, right?

00:27:17.360 --> 00:27:19.890
Like, this isn't rocket science
about how this is working.

00:27:19.890 --> 00:27:22.430
It's straightforward.

00:27:22.430 --> 00:27:25.760
But by doing that, and you
can see by using bullets,

00:27:25.760 --> 00:27:26.730
we can indent.

00:27:26.730 --> 00:27:30.170
So if it gets complicated,
that can just match--

00:27:30.170 --> 00:27:32.313
it can match the indentation
you would see in code.

00:27:32.313 --> 00:27:33.980
It matches that with
bullets, so you see

00:27:33.980 --> 00:27:36.050
that all kind of works out OK.

00:27:36.050 --> 00:27:38.090
And in fact, this
is a complex formula

00:27:38.090 --> 00:27:40.770
that is actually kind of
hard to read, even in code.

00:27:40.770 --> 00:27:41.910
And this is kind of nice.

00:27:41.910 --> 00:27:43.452
But this is something
that an analyst

00:27:43.452 --> 00:27:45.100
can read without knowing SQL.

00:27:45.100 --> 00:27:45.600
OK.

00:27:45.600 --> 00:27:46.340
If one of these--

00:27:46.340 --> 00:27:48.240
I see all this
Australia stuff there,

00:27:48.240 --> 00:27:49.910
then it's Australia,
New Zealand.

00:27:49.910 --> 00:27:51.120
Gotcha.

00:27:51.120 --> 00:27:51.620
OK.

00:27:51.620 --> 00:27:54.050
If they're in the US,
but also if they're

00:27:54.050 --> 00:27:58.347
in one of these states in the
US, or later on, it says--

00:27:58.347 --> 00:27:59.930
or maybe they're in
Canada, et cetera,

00:27:59.930 --> 00:28:02.982
then they're in the
North America East Group.

00:28:02.982 --> 00:28:04.440
But if I'm in sales,
and I'm going,

00:28:04.440 --> 00:28:05.450
wait a minute,
how did you decide

00:28:05.450 --> 00:28:06.620
who's in North America East?

00:28:06.620 --> 00:28:08.120
It's super easy to
just go see that.

00:28:08.120 --> 00:28:10.640
And if it's wrong, by the
way, easy to see and go

00:28:10.640 --> 00:28:13.058
tell the developer,
oh, this city

00:28:13.058 --> 00:28:14.600
is supposed to be
in the other thing,

00:28:14.600 --> 00:28:16.340
and very easy for
the developer to fix.

00:28:16.340 --> 00:28:18.380
But without that, good
luck ever finding that.

00:28:18.380 --> 00:28:20.015
You're going to maybe find it.

00:28:20.015 --> 00:28:21.180
It's very hard.

00:28:21.180 --> 00:28:22.940
But just by making
it easier to read,

00:28:22.940 --> 00:28:25.070
and because we have the
stuff in the JSON format,

00:28:25.070 --> 00:28:27.880
it's super easy to make those.

00:28:27.880 --> 00:28:29.540
Otherwise, that'd be hard.

00:28:29.540 --> 00:28:33.560
So the stuff on the right
is more like for auditing.

00:28:33.560 --> 00:28:37.670
So this is just different
tabs that we have in the HTML.

00:28:37.670 --> 00:28:38.960
And this one we call origins.

00:28:38.960 --> 00:28:42.230
And the idea is, all right,
I've got an expression.

00:28:42.230 --> 00:28:47.690
And what are all the systems
of records and all the fields

00:28:47.690 --> 00:28:49.640
in those systems of
record that were touched

00:28:49.640 --> 00:28:52.370
in the making of
this final result,

00:28:52.370 --> 00:28:54.950
recursively, like
everywhere, what got touched?

00:28:54.950 --> 00:28:57.860
So it's just a big ol' list,
sorted by where it came from,

00:28:57.860 --> 00:28:59.810
but it's a big ol' list.

00:28:59.810 --> 00:29:03.438
That may not seem that useful
as an engineer, because it's

00:29:03.438 --> 00:29:05.480
useful to not flatten
things out for an engineer.

00:29:05.480 --> 00:29:06.830
It's good to chunk things.

00:29:06.830 --> 00:29:09.140
But if you're an
auditor, this is great.

00:29:09.140 --> 00:29:12.620
Are you sure PII wasn't used
in the making of this field?

00:29:12.620 --> 00:29:15.120
Well, that's all the data that
was used in making the field.

00:29:15.120 --> 00:29:18.320
So if one of these needs to
be PII, we can go mark it.

00:29:18.320 --> 00:29:20.090
And then this will
say PII, no problem.

00:29:22.535 --> 00:29:24.410
But, on the other hand,
if this is all clean,

00:29:24.410 --> 00:29:25.702
an auditor can go, yeah, right.

00:29:25.702 --> 00:29:27.140
This is all clean.

00:29:27.140 --> 00:29:28.740
We're good.

00:29:28.740 --> 00:29:30.310
Again, as an auditor,
this is huge.

00:29:30.310 --> 00:29:32.352
And if you're in finance,
if the auditor's happy,

00:29:32.352 --> 00:29:33.540
then you're happy.

00:29:33.540 --> 00:29:34.650
That's it.

00:29:34.650 --> 00:29:37.737
And even as a developer, the
auditor comes to you and says,

00:29:37.737 --> 00:29:39.570
you need to document
this, and explain that.

00:29:39.570 --> 00:29:40.800
That's annoying.

00:29:40.800 --> 00:29:42.420
To be able to hand
this over and say,

00:29:42.420 --> 00:29:44.003
let me know if you
have any questions,

00:29:44.003 --> 00:29:45.698
is awesome, as an
engineer, that you

00:29:45.698 --> 00:29:46.990
don't have to do that yourself.

00:29:46.990 --> 00:29:50.460
So it may not seem
amazing, but it is

00:29:50.460 --> 00:29:51.960
for these use cases for data.

00:29:54.860 --> 00:29:58.182
So then, not everyone
wants to consume SQL.

00:29:58.182 --> 00:29:59.890
Actually, a lot of
data analysts nowadays

00:29:59.890 --> 00:30:02.447
do want to consume SQL
directly, and that's great.

00:30:02.447 --> 00:30:04.030
And so for those
that don't, we wanted

00:30:04.030 --> 00:30:07.690
to pick a standardized
tool for the company,

00:30:07.690 --> 00:30:09.640
for people to build
reports and such.

00:30:09.640 --> 00:30:10.940
And we picked Looker.

00:30:10.940 --> 00:30:14.403
There's obviously a lot of good
ones to pick from nowadays.

00:30:14.403 --> 00:30:15.820
And everybody's
going to integrate

00:30:15.820 --> 00:30:17.028
with BigQuery, which is nice.

00:30:17.028 --> 00:30:19.540
It's another reason to pick
one of the winners in the data

00:30:19.540 --> 00:30:25.000
warehousing world to maximize
your options for integrations.

00:30:25.000 --> 00:30:26.660
So we picked Looker.

00:30:26.660 --> 00:30:28.630
There are a couple of
reasons, just if you're

00:30:28.630 --> 00:30:29.873
curious why we didn't pick--

00:30:29.873 --> 00:30:30.790
and it's not like we--

00:30:30.790 --> 00:30:32.290
I don't really care
which one you pick.

00:30:32.290 --> 00:30:33.457
I'm not shilling for Looker.

00:30:33.457 --> 00:30:39.240
But in case this is useful
to you, Looker caches data.

00:30:39.240 --> 00:30:42.790
And that means it's both faster
for the end user and cheaper.

00:30:42.790 --> 00:30:44.807
Because BigQuery,
of course, charges

00:30:44.807 --> 00:30:47.140
for storage, which generally
isn't that much, unless you

00:30:47.140 --> 00:30:50.200
have all the records that
we have, and for query time.

00:30:50.200 --> 00:30:51.700
And so if you cache,
then that means

00:30:51.700 --> 00:30:55.210
you're even less worried about
what Google's charging you.

00:30:55.210 --> 00:30:58.780
Another thing is, Looker
makes it very easy

00:30:58.780 --> 00:31:01.300
to build reports in which you
can filter and double-click.

00:31:01.300 --> 00:31:02.675
And there's
something people love

00:31:02.675 --> 00:31:05.080
about being able to drill
down by double-clicking.

00:31:05.080 --> 00:31:06.400
They love to drill down.

00:31:06.400 --> 00:31:07.792
And Looker-- I don't know.

00:31:07.792 --> 00:31:08.500
It makes it easy.

00:31:08.500 --> 00:31:10.445
People love that stuff.

00:31:10.445 --> 00:31:12.820
The other thing is, whatever
you're looking at in Looker,

00:31:12.820 --> 00:31:15.310
with all the filters and the
drilldowns and the things--

00:31:15.310 --> 00:31:17.710
is all in the getURL.

00:31:17.710 --> 00:31:19.870
So you can just bookmark
or throw that URL

00:31:19.870 --> 00:31:20.870
at somebody or whatever.

00:31:20.870 --> 00:31:23.918
And they can leap right
into that particular thing.

00:31:23.918 --> 00:31:25.960
And that's not true of
some of these other tools.

00:31:25.960 --> 00:31:27.670
That's just so
handy, in practice,

00:31:27.670 --> 00:31:29.120
to be able to do that.

00:31:29.120 --> 00:31:32.250
Or if you're building
dashboards-- so I'm an analyst.

00:31:32.250 --> 00:31:35.830
And I have three dashboards
I build for my function.

00:31:35.830 --> 00:31:37.870
I mean, for me to
be able to just say,

00:31:37.870 --> 00:31:40.920
oh, I'll have three tabs, and
each one is just this URL.

00:31:40.920 --> 00:31:43.240
And updating what's in it
is just updating the URL.

00:31:43.240 --> 00:31:44.170
How easy is this?

00:31:44.170 --> 00:31:45.670
It's super easy.

00:31:45.670 --> 00:31:48.970
Or people on their laptops
can just bookmark a URL.

00:31:48.970 --> 00:31:51.130
It becomes really easy to do.

00:31:51.130 --> 00:31:54.198
Also, all the things that
are in there are embeddable.

00:31:54.198 --> 00:31:55.990
So, for example, there's
a lot of this kind

00:31:55.990 --> 00:31:59.170
of data we wanted to
put into Salesforce,

00:31:59.170 --> 00:32:01.240
into the UI of Salesforce.

00:32:01.240 --> 00:32:04.020
Because if you're in the sales
team, you live in Salesforce.

00:32:04.020 --> 00:32:06.312
You don't want to live in
Salesforce and some reporting

00:32:06.312 --> 00:32:07.570
tool and be jumping around.

00:32:07.570 --> 00:32:09.280
That's not good.

00:32:09.280 --> 00:32:12.820
So with Looker, we're able to
take these reports, or charts,

00:32:12.820 --> 00:32:14.020
or whatever kinds of things.

00:32:14.020 --> 00:32:16.270
And just a little
piece, like one chart

00:32:16.270 --> 00:32:19.600
with this filter on it of
the past week, or whenever--

00:32:19.600 --> 00:32:21.940
and you can embed that
right into the dashboard

00:32:21.940 --> 00:32:24.490
in Salesforce with
no code in Salesforce

00:32:24.490 --> 00:32:26.230
and no special crap,
no special add-ons.

00:32:26.230 --> 00:32:28.780
You can just embed it,
again, in part, because

00:32:28.780 --> 00:32:31.630
of this deep linking
that goes right in.

00:32:31.630 --> 00:32:34.420
So that means we're able
to build, the sales team--

00:32:34.420 --> 00:32:36.460
I should say, the
sales ops team is

00:32:36.460 --> 00:32:38.860
able to build
dashboards in Salesforce

00:32:38.860 --> 00:32:40.150
for the reps and the managers.

00:32:40.150 --> 00:32:42.730
But they never have to
leave Salesforce to get

00:32:42.730 --> 00:32:44.380
this data, which is awesome.

00:32:44.380 --> 00:32:46.078
Again, that's Looker.

00:32:46.078 --> 00:32:47.620
But they still have
the double-click.

00:32:47.620 --> 00:32:49.453
So if they go, well,
what about this bullet?

00:32:49.453 --> 00:32:51.430
Now, they're in Looker,
and they can go play.

00:32:51.430 --> 00:32:54.370
So it's just, again, a
really nice thing for people.

00:32:54.370 --> 00:32:56.860
And again, as engineers,
sometimes you think of this,

00:32:56.860 --> 00:32:58.028
like, who cares?

00:32:58.028 --> 00:33:00.320
So it's embedded in this
dashboard instead of that one.

00:33:00.320 --> 00:33:01.720
Or so what, you can click on it?

00:33:01.720 --> 00:33:03.410
It doesn't sound
like a big deal,

00:33:03.410 --> 00:33:06.295
but in practice, especially for
non-technical people, the idea

00:33:06.295 --> 00:33:08.170
that it's just there,
and I can click and get

00:33:08.170 --> 00:33:11.968
more is actually a really
big usability factor.

00:33:11.968 --> 00:33:14.260
And that's what this part of
the system should be about

00:33:14.260 --> 00:33:17.380
is usability for the folks
that need to use the data.

00:33:17.380 --> 00:33:20.050
So that's what we picked.

00:33:20.050 --> 00:33:22.960
So a couple of things--

00:33:22.960 --> 00:33:25.300
it was harder to connect to
all the systems of record

00:33:25.300 --> 00:33:29.230
than we thought, even
with things like Segment.

00:33:29.230 --> 00:33:31.943
We actually had to file and get
some bugs fixed from Segment

00:33:31.943 --> 00:33:33.610
just to get our
Salesforce data to work.

00:33:33.610 --> 00:33:36.530
So it wasn't easy.

00:33:36.530 --> 00:33:39.490
We also ran into some
trickiness with secrets.

00:33:39.490 --> 00:33:42.880
So for example, we
used Apache Airflow

00:33:42.880 --> 00:33:47.450
to create a workflow around
how to pull all this data.

00:33:47.450 --> 00:33:49.825
So in other words, pull
it from all these systems,

00:33:49.825 --> 00:33:51.700
and then make these
transformations, and then

00:33:51.700 --> 00:33:53.347
these, and the whole series--

00:33:53.347 --> 00:33:55.930
we used Apache Airflow-- it's a
workflow system-- for defining

00:33:55.930 --> 00:33:57.040
those and doing it.

00:33:57.040 --> 00:33:59.767
Once again, how did
we create those rules?

00:33:59.767 --> 00:34:01.600
We got it from the JSON,
and we spit it out,

00:34:01.600 --> 00:34:03.105
and it goes right into Airflow.

00:34:03.105 --> 00:34:04.480
And Google has a
managed Airflow,

00:34:04.480 --> 00:34:05.930
by the way, which we use.

00:34:05.930 --> 00:34:07.947
And then it runs those
things on Kubernetes.

00:34:07.947 --> 00:34:10.030
So then we were able to
use the Kubernetes Secrets

00:34:10.030 --> 00:34:12.465
system to actually take
all those Secrets to all

00:34:12.465 --> 00:34:13.840
those systems--
which, of course,

00:34:13.840 --> 00:34:15.090
those are crown jewel secrets.

00:34:15.090 --> 00:34:16.380
So that's really careful.

00:34:16.380 --> 00:34:20.120
Our financial data, our customer
data-- this is a scary stuff.

00:34:20.120 --> 00:34:22.659
But by being able to put it
into the vault in Kubernetes

00:34:22.659 --> 00:34:24.402
and letting it
run in there, it's

00:34:24.402 --> 00:34:26.860
a nice, safe way where no human
being ever gets their mitts

00:34:26.860 --> 00:34:28.270
on those secrets.

00:34:28.270 --> 00:34:31.389
And yet, we can automate
the pulling of all the data.

00:34:31.389 --> 00:34:32.900
So that's really nice.

00:34:32.900 --> 00:34:35.920
So it was hard to get
that to work, actually.

00:34:35.920 --> 00:34:37.750
It was non-trivial
to figure out how

00:34:37.750 --> 00:34:39.701
to get multiple things
into the vault, how

00:34:39.701 --> 00:34:42.159
to make sure the Kubernetes
got the stuff out of the vault.

00:34:42.159 --> 00:34:44.080
These things were not
easy for us to get up

00:34:44.080 --> 00:34:45.310
and running at first.

00:34:45.310 --> 00:34:47.020
But once they were
running, it's now

00:34:47.020 --> 00:34:50.560
been running for
maybe 3 and 1/2 months

00:34:50.560 --> 00:34:54.130
with not one problem, no
maintenance issue, no nothing

00:34:54.130 --> 00:34:54.820
at all.

00:34:54.820 --> 00:34:56.300
So that's really good.

00:34:56.300 --> 00:34:59.050
So overall, it was really
good, even though it

00:34:59.050 --> 00:35:01.720
was a challenge to get going.

00:35:01.720 --> 00:35:03.160
It's still difficult,
to this day,

00:35:03.160 --> 00:35:04.750
to understand what
data is right.

00:35:04.750 --> 00:35:08.980
And I think, even though
we've built a lot of systems

00:35:08.980 --> 00:35:10.360
for that, it's still hard.

00:35:10.360 --> 00:35:14.207
So for example, we'll pull
data from our general ledger.

00:35:14.207 --> 00:35:16.540
We'll pull data from Stripe
where we run the credit card

00:35:16.540 --> 00:35:19.300
side of the business
and from the user

00:35:19.300 --> 00:35:21.072
portal where we show invoices.

00:35:21.072 --> 00:35:23.530
And there will be customers
where that stuff doesn't match.

00:35:23.530 --> 00:35:25.630
And you say, OK.

00:35:25.630 --> 00:35:27.910
But all this stuff is
supposed to be right.

00:35:27.910 --> 00:35:30.970
And at that point,
Google can't help you.

00:35:30.970 --> 00:35:33.040
And JSON can't help you.

00:35:33.040 --> 00:35:36.040
And so just running through
all that has taken--

00:35:36.040 --> 00:35:38.170
and we continue to work
on that stuff today--

00:35:38.170 --> 00:35:41.440
it has taken a lot more
time than we expected.

00:35:41.440 --> 00:35:43.780
When we've talked
to other folks,

00:35:43.780 --> 00:35:47.320
even in the server business,
about billing and those kinds

00:35:47.320 --> 00:35:50.200
of areas, we discovered that
Google has this problem.

00:35:50.200 --> 00:35:51.460
Amazon has this problem.

00:35:51.460 --> 00:35:52.960
Rackspace had this problem.

00:35:52.960 --> 00:35:56.290
Actually, billing turns out to
be really hard, but who knew?

00:35:56.290 --> 00:35:57.880
But actually, it's really hard.

00:35:57.880 --> 00:36:01.150
So at least, though, we have a
system where it's easy for us

00:36:01.150 --> 00:36:03.850
to understand what it is that
we are doing in the first place

00:36:03.850 --> 00:36:05.933
and, therefore, maybe
iterate and diagnose.

00:36:05.933 --> 00:36:07.600
The other thing we
did is we made tests.

00:36:07.600 --> 00:36:09.490
Obviously, unit tests,
integration tests-- everybody

00:36:09.490 --> 00:36:10.030
knows that.

00:36:10.030 --> 00:36:11.320
So of course, we did that.

00:36:11.320 --> 00:36:14.900
The other thing that we did is
these spot-check smoke tests.

00:36:14.900 --> 00:36:17.320
So what we'd do is we'd say,
we'd discover some customer

00:36:17.320 --> 00:36:20.500
with weird data,
and so, OK, we would

00:36:20.500 --> 00:36:24.790
create a CSV file of the
result of some report

00:36:24.790 --> 00:36:26.830
against that customer
that was actually right.

00:36:26.830 --> 00:36:28.630
And that became a test, right?

00:36:28.630 --> 00:36:31.750
So every time we update
all the corporate data,

00:36:31.750 --> 00:36:34.000
we'll run this particular
report and check it

00:36:34.000 --> 00:36:36.985
against the test CSV to make
sure that we didn't break that.

00:36:36.985 --> 00:36:38.860
So it's like an integration
test in that it's

00:36:38.860 --> 00:36:41.750
an end-to-end test, but it's
on the actual production data.

00:36:41.750 --> 00:36:43.257
So it's like a third
level of tests.

00:36:43.257 --> 00:36:45.340
So of course, in those,
we have to be careful just

00:36:45.340 --> 00:36:47.805
to read and validate and
never change anything,

00:36:47.805 --> 00:36:49.430
unlike an integration
test where you're

00:36:49.430 --> 00:36:52.810
allowed to change the state,
because you can reset it.

00:36:52.810 --> 00:36:55.630
So by having just the system--
and we've probably all built

00:36:55.630 --> 00:36:58.900
these kinds of things where you
can drop a CSV file somewhere

00:36:58.900 --> 00:37:02.147
and drop a query somewhere
and add a test easily--

00:37:02.147 --> 00:37:04.730
because of that, we've been able
to add about 100 tests, which

00:37:04.730 --> 00:37:05.772
doesn't sound like a lot.

00:37:05.772 --> 00:37:09.250
But we have maybe 500
or 600 unit tests.

00:37:09.250 --> 00:37:11.150
But we were able to
add an end-to-end test.

00:37:11.150 --> 00:37:13.060
So now, even when
we find something

00:37:13.060 --> 00:37:16.450
really strange in a boundary,
and we make a little change

00:37:16.450 --> 00:37:18.778
to the JSON, we're very
confident, after it

00:37:18.778 --> 00:37:20.320
gets deployed, that
we haven't broken

00:37:20.320 --> 00:37:23.800
anything in the other crazy
cases that we've seen, right?

00:37:23.800 --> 00:37:27.490
So taking the time to build
those in-production tests,

00:37:27.490 --> 00:37:31.660
that has been useful
in this other problem.

00:37:31.660 --> 00:37:35.140
So we've changed things as a
result. These are some of them.

00:37:35.140 --> 00:37:36.390
I'll give you a funny example.

00:37:39.270 --> 00:37:42.090
You would think that the
number of customers we have

00:37:42.090 --> 00:37:44.530
is not a debatable
or difficult concept,

00:37:44.530 --> 00:37:46.740
but actually it
is, it turns out.

00:37:46.740 --> 00:37:49.890
Everybody has a different number
for how many customers we have.

00:37:49.890 --> 00:37:51.870
And this has solved
that just by normalizing

00:37:51.870 --> 00:37:52.870
what we call a customer.

00:37:52.870 --> 00:37:56.970
So, for example, in finance,
if someone comes in on April 10

00:37:56.970 --> 00:37:59.160
and leaves on April 12,
and we give them a refund,

00:37:59.160 --> 00:38:03.270
as we do, then, as far
as GAAP is concerned,

00:38:03.270 --> 00:38:04.140
no money happened.

00:38:04.140 --> 00:38:06.820
No money changed hands in total.

00:38:06.820 --> 00:38:10.440
And so this is a no-op,
and so it's not a customer.

00:38:10.440 --> 00:38:14.220
So finance doesn't count that
as a customer for the month.

00:38:14.220 --> 00:38:16.250
In support, that's
not how they view it.

00:38:16.250 --> 00:38:18.120
In support, someone
came in and called them.

00:38:18.120 --> 00:38:20.400
So obviously, it's a customer.

00:38:20.400 --> 00:38:21.180
I talked to them.

00:38:21.180 --> 00:38:22.890
It must have been a customer.

00:38:22.890 --> 00:38:24.350
Who else was I talking to?

00:38:24.350 --> 00:38:24.960
So it counts.

00:38:24.960 --> 00:38:27.870
And so when support's
thinking, OK, I'm

00:38:27.870 --> 00:38:31.440
trying to do capacity planning
and hiring planning, and how

00:38:31.440 --> 00:38:34.072
many customers we get a month,
and, therefore, how many people

00:38:34.072 --> 00:38:35.280
I need, and blah, blah, blah.

00:38:35.280 --> 00:38:38.070
And we have about 250 or
300 people in support.

00:38:38.070 --> 00:38:41.010
So this planning is important.

00:38:41.010 --> 00:38:42.810
And getting the details
right is important

00:38:42.810 --> 00:38:45.612
because it's a lot of folks
that we might need to hire.

00:38:45.612 --> 00:38:47.195
They need to count
that as a customer,

00:38:47.195 --> 00:38:49.362
or they're not going to get
their hiring plan right.

00:38:49.362 --> 00:38:51.210
And similarly, finance
needs to, I guess,

00:38:51.210 --> 00:38:53.880
not count them as a customer,
so that we're correct with GAAP.

00:38:53.880 --> 00:38:57.510
So the point being,
by forcing ourselves

00:38:57.510 --> 00:39:00.390
to see all of this stuff,
even different definitions

00:39:00.390 --> 00:39:02.900
of the same concept,
like a customer,

00:39:02.900 --> 00:39:06.160
it forced us to go, OK, what
do we really mean by this?

00:39:06.160 --> 00:39:07.650
What do we need to mean?

00:39:07.650 --> 00:39:10.620
We might need the concept
of a GAAP customer,

00:39:10.620 --> 00:39:11.790
or a real customer.

00:39:11.790 --> 00:39:13.350
We might need
different words, even.

00:39:13.350 --> 00:39:14.940
And with all the
data in one place,

00:39:14.940 --> 00:39:18.180
we can produce all
of those numbers.

00:39:18.180 --> 00:39:20.400
So everyone can get the data
that they actually need.

00:39:20.400 --> 00:39:22.903
But we can actually normalize
what we're talking about.

00:39:22.903 --> 00:39:24.570
So if you think it's
bad for a customer,

00:39:24.570 --> 00:39:29.040
you can imagine things that are
less simple, how confusing that

00:39:29.040 --> 00:39:31.500
was getting.

00:39:31.500 --> 00:39:33.720
Anyway, so it's
helped us normalize

00:39:33.720 --> 00:39:35.220
how we talk about
things and report

00:39:35.220 --> 00:39:36.803
on things, which has
been really nice,

00:39:36.803 --> 00:39:38.560
and other things like this.

00:39:38.560 --> 00:39:39.060
OK.

00:39:39.060 --> 00:39:40.050
Well, I guess
we'll call it then.

00:39:40.050 --> 00:39:40.780
Thanks, everybody.

00:39:40.780 --> 00:39:41.655
Thanks for your time.

00:39:41.655 --> 00:39:44.030
[MUSIC PLAYING]

